# Erdős Problem #99 — zero-Hall-charge strain audit

## Exact finite lemma

Let (I) be a finite index set, let (z_i) be points of the unit triangular
lattice, and let

\[
Y=\{z_i:i\in I\},
\qquad
X=\{s z_i+c:i\in I\},
\qquad s>1.
\]

Assume (X) is unit-separated and

\[
|s z_i+c-z_i|<1qquad(i\in I).
\]

Then the identity map (s z_i+c\mapsto z_i) is a strict-conflict matching from X
to Y.  Since the two shores have the same cardinality,

\[
\nu(X,Y)=|I|,
\qquad
\max_{Q\subseteq X}\bigl(|Q|-|N_Y(Q)|\bigr)=0.
\]

The second equality is the ordinary matching deficiency formula.  No geometric
optimality is being assumed.

## Why this matters

For a square index patch (I=\{0,\ldots,L-1\}^2), take

\[
s=1+\frac{\alpha}{L},
\qquad c=0,
\]

with (0<\alpha<1/\sqrt3).  Every (X)-point is a scaled lattice point, so X has
no unit edges and no unit equilateral triangles.  Y is an unscaled lattice patch and
contains many unit equilateral triangles.  Yet the strict-conflict graph has a
perfect identity matching, because the displacement

\[
|(s-1)z_i|\le \frac{\alpha}{L}\sqrt3(L-1)<1.
\]

Thus an (O(1/L)) distributed strain can erase every exact unit contact on the
source side while leaving the source-to-lattice Hall deficiency exactly zero.  If
one adds one extra lattice point to Y, the same matching gives deficiency exactly at
most one; the output of [`capacity_flag.py`](../../research/capacity_flag.py) records
both values for (L=8, \alpha=1/4).

This is a finite, exact explanation for why local monomer purity, triangle-freeness,
and bounded-displacement matching cannot by themselves prove the Target A inequality

\[
h(G)\ge j.
\]

The missing ingredient must still be global diameter optimality or an exterior/
boundary constraint that invalidates the identity matching.  In particular, the
calculation is not a counterexample to Erdős Problem #99: the strained patch is not
claimed to be a globally diameter-minimizing configuration.
